ent chips.].\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': "Evaluation: Let me analyze this situation carefully.\n\n**Current Hand Analysis:**\n- My cards: 7♥, 6♣\n- Board: 4♦, 9♠, 2♦, 3♣\n- This is the Turn (4 community cards are out)\n\n**Hand Strength:**\nI have 7-high with no made hand currently. However, I have an open-ended straight draw:\n- I need a 5 to complete a straight (3-4-5-6-7)\n- There are 4 fives in the deck, giving me 4 outs\n- With one card to come (the River), my probability is approximately 4/46 ≈ 8.7%\n\n**Position & Action History:**\n- Both players have just checked through Pre-flop, Flop, and Turn\n- Opponent checked again on the Turn\n- This passive play suggests neither player has a strong hand\n- Opponent likely doesn't have a pair of 9s or better, otherwise they'd bet for value\n\n**Stack Considerations:**\n- I have 19,900 chips (slightly below starting stack)\n- I'm down 430 chips cumulatively across previous hands\n- This is hand 8 of 20, so still early-mid game\n\n**Strategic Options:**\n\n1. **Check (0)**: Safe play, see the river for free. Given we both have weak hands, this is reasonable.\n\n2. **Fold (-1)**: No reason to fold when checking is free and I have outs.\n\n3. **Bet/Raise (100+)**: Betting here as a semi-bluff could work since opponent has shown weakness. A bet of 100-200 chips might take down the pot immediately, and if called, I still have outs.\n\n4. **All-in (-2)**: Way too aggressive for a draw with only 8.7% equity.\n\n**Best Play:**\nA small bet (around 100-150) makes sense as a semi-bluff. The opponent has checked three streets showing extreme weakness. Even with 7-high, there's a good chance they fold to any aggression. If called, I still have my straight outs.\n\nHowever, given the pot is likely very small (just blinds), and I have a legitimate draw, checking to see a free river card is also sound, especially since I'm behind cumulatively and should avoid unnecessary risks with weak holdings.\n\nReason: While a small bet as a